<TopicPage slug="subjects/anthropology/intermediate/human-evolution-a-timeline">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Human evolution is <strong>seven million years of experiments, of which we are the only
    survivor</strong>. The fossil record plus genetics (which now dates splits independently)
    tell one of science's great stories — and it keeps being rewritten as new fossils and ancient
    genomes arrive.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="seven million years in four acts" widget="equationStep" config={evoCfg} />

  <h2>What the last decade changed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ancient DNA rewrote the tree:</strong> Svante Pääbo's sequencing (Nobel 2022) discovered the Denisovans from a finger bone, mapped interbreeding, and turned "replacement vs continuity" debates into measurable ancestry fractions.</li>
    <li><strong>Homo naledi (2015):</strong> small-brained, recent (~300 kya), deep in a cave — apparently buried deliberately? The find scrambled the "big brain = advanced behaviour" assumption.</li>
    <li><strong>Tools before brains, brains before sapience:</strong> the sequence overturned the Victorian ladder — every "first" (tools, fire, art) has moved earlier and earlier as the record improves.</li>
    <li><strong>Interbreeding was normal:</strong> sapiens, Neanderthals and Denisovans mixed repeatedly — the clean species-boundary story was wrong; modern Melanesians carry ~5% Denisovan DNA (and high-altitude adaptation in Tibetans comes from that introgression).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hublin et al. (2017); Pääbo, Nobel lecture (2022); Stringer & Andrews; the fossil hominin record ( Smithsonian Human Origins).</p>
</TopicPage>
